  "query_preview": "..Current radar depicts a wide swath of rainfall\nongoing across much of central and southern Utah generally east of \nthe I-15 corridor. As instability has waned this evening, embedded \nconvection has eased, taking on a more stratiform mode across most \nof the area. Despite being more stratiform in nature, the deep \ntropical moisture in place has allowed for rainfall rates to remain \nquite efficient, which will exacerbate impacts in areas already \nexperiencing ongoing flooding. This swath of rainfall will decrease \nin coverage as it continues to slowly track northeastward overnight \nbefore tapering off over central Utah mid-Tuesday morning.\n\nThe associated upper-level low continues to dig further southeast \ninto Arizona on Tuesday, with far southern Utah remaining in a \nfavorable area for wrap-around moisture. Ample instability will \nstill exist on the northern periphery of the low, allowing widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms to develop Tuesday afternoon \nacross southern Utah. Heaviest rainfall is favored across the higher \nterrain given easterly flow resulting in upslope enhancement, and \nareas near the Arizona border. Given widespread heavy rainfall on \nMonday has primed soils across the area, the flash flood risk will \nremain elevated into Tuesday evening across much of southern Utah. \n\nNorthern and central Utah will remain under the influence of dry, \nnortherly flow on Tuesday, resulting in cooler temperatures and \nlimited chances for precipitation. This dry airmass will push into \nsouthern Utah overnight into Wednesday morning, ushering out any \nlingering precipitation. Models still show a strong consensus in \nlongwave ridging developing across the western US mid-to-late week, \nindicative of a gradual warming and drying trend through this period.",
